                <h2 class="font-display text-base font-bold mb-3">On this page</h2>
                <ol class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-1 gap-x-6 gap-y-2.5 text-sm">
                  {toc.map((t, i) => (
                    <li>
                      <a href={`#${t.id}`} class="text-[#e00e10] hover:underline">
                        <span class="text-black/60 tabular-nums mr-1">{i + 1}.</span>{t.label}
                      </a>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ol>
              </nav>
            </aside>

            <div class="prose prose-neutral max-w-none prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:scroll-mt-28 prose-a:text-[#e00e10] prose-a:underline prose-a:decoration-[#e00e10]/40 prose-a:underline-offset-2 hover:prose-a:decoration-[#e00e10] prose-table:text-sm prose-h2:mt-12 prose-h2:pt-8 prose-h2:border-t prose-h2:border-black/10 first:prose-h2:mt-0 first:prose-h2:pt-0 first:prose-h2:border-0">

        <p>
          Nobody puts "smells faintly of someone else's cigarettes" in a listing, which is how you
          find out at the valet stand.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-smell">What the smell tells you</h2>

        <p>
          Smoke is the one thing in a cabin you cannot photograph, which is exactly why it is worth
          leading with. It is also the hardest to undo. Residue settles into the headliner, the
          vents and the seat foam, and it does not leave on its own — the
          {' '}<a href="https://www.epa.gov/indoor-air-quality-iaq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPA's
          work on indoor air quality</a> is blunt about how long it lingers in soft materials. Once
          a car has it, every renter after that has it too.
        </p>

        <p>
          So it is a useful test, because keeping it out is not a detailing problem. It is a policy
          problem. It means turning down bookings, and a company renting on volume cannot afford to
          turn down bookings. That is the whole mechanism, and the smell is just where you notice it.
        </p>

        <p>
          What we do about it is two steps rather than one. The car is detailed between every
          rental, and once that is finished we run an ozone treatment through the cabin, which
          oxidises the residue sitting in the headliner and the vents — the part a wipe-down never
          reaches. The catch, and the reason this is worth explaining, is that ozone only works on a
          car that has already been cleaned properly. Run it over a dirty cabin and it masks the
          smell for about a week instead of removing it. So the order matters, and the detail has to
          be real before the machine goes anywhere near it.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is also why keeping it out is ultimately a policy problem rather than an equipment
          one. It means turning down bookings from anyone who intends to smoke in the car, and a
          company renting on volume cannot afford to turn down bookings. The machine is the easy
          part. The decision is not.
        </p>

        <p>
          The same logic runs through the rest of a cabin. Alcantara on a {sf90.name} seat shows
          every mark it has ever taken. A white diamond-quilted rear bench shows more. Those
          materials are either maintained constantly or they look tired within a season, and you can
          tell which within about four seconds of opening the door.
        </p>

        <figure class="not-prose my-10">
          <img
            src={bodySrc}
            srcset={bodySrcSet}
            sizes="(min-width: 1140px) 760px, 100vw"
            width="1000"
            height="667"
            alt={bodyAlt}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
