            no third option.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 id="volume">Why cheap rates wear cars out</h2>

        <p>
          A lot of companies undercut the market here, and the cars are often not as well kept,
          because of the sheer volume they have to push to make the numbers work. That is not a
          complaint about competitors — it is arithmetic. A lower daily rate needs more rental days
          to return the same money. More days means more handovers, more miles, and less time
          between bookings to put anything right.
        </p>

        <p>
          The discount is real. It is just funded by the condition of the car. You are not getting
          the same product for less; you are getting a more tired version of it, and the saving
          shows up as the thing you came for being slightly less worth buying.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our rates run from {usd(s580.price)} a day for the {s580.make} {s580.name} up to
          {' '}{usd(sf90.price)} for the {sf90.name}, and the published number is the same for you
          as for anyone. The full{' '}
          <a href="/pricing">rate card, deposit and policies</a> sit on one page rather than being
          held back for the phone call.
        </p>

        <figure class="not-prose my-10 rounded-2xl border border-black/10 bg-[#fafafa] p-6">
          <blockquote class="text-[#0a0a0a]/80 leading-relaxed">
            <p>“{testimonial.text}”</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption class="mt-4 text-sm text-[#0a0a0a]/60">
            <span class="text-[#e00e10]" aria-hidden="true">★★★★★</span>
            <span class="font-semibold text-[#0a0a0a]"> {testimonial.name}</span> — verified Google review
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 id="owner-operated">Owner-operated, not investor-operated</h2>

        <p>
          Most of this industry is structured the other way round. Someone buys a car as an
          investment, hands it to a management company, and the people doing handovers are paid per
          handover. Nobody in that chain personally absorbs the cost of a cabin that needs three
          hours of work, so it gets two.
        </p>

        <p>
          I set the prices, I pay for the detailing, and I hand over the keys. That is not a
          marketing position, it is just the structure of the business, and it has one practical
          consequence: there is no desk between the decision and whoever lives with it. When a car
          needs putting right before the next booking, the person deciding whether that is worth
          doing is the person whose name is on the reviews.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is also why the cheaper car gets recommended here more often than you would expect. The
          {' '}<a href="/cars/mercedes-s580">{s580.make} {s580.name}</a> at {usd(s580.price)} is
          genuinely the right call for a lot of bookings, and saying so costs us margin on the day
          and keeps the customer. An operator makes that trade. A management fee does not.
        </p>

        <h2 id="check">What to check before you book, anywhere</h2>

        <p>
          None of this is worth anything if you cannot verify it, so here is what to actually look
          at — on this site or any other.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Are the photographs of that exact car?</strong> Most listings show a stock image of the model. Ours are the car that arrives, which is also why we have to keep it looking like that.</li>
          <li><strong>Is the deposit published?</strong> Ours is {deposit.display}, flat on every car. Miami's range runs far wider, and most companies publish nothing until you are committed.</li>
          <li><strong>Is mileage pooled or capped daily?</strong> Ours pools — {perDay} a day across the whole booking, so three days is {perDay * 3} miles used however you like. A daily cap that resets at midnight is a materially worse deal at the same headline price.</li>
          <li><strong>What is the minimum age?</strong> {minimumAge} here, on every car, with a valid licence and qualifying insurance. Florida's requirements are set by the{' '}
            <a href="https://www.flhsmv.gov/driver-licenses-id-cards/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Department of Highway Safety and Motor Vehicles</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Who hands you the keys?</strong> Ask. The answer tells you more about how the car has been treated than any photograph will.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Delivery is {deliveryEachWaySummary}, to a hotel, a residence or either airport, and
          longer bookings cost less per day: {discountSummary}. You can see the whole{' '}
          <a href="/fleet">fleet and what each car costs</a> in one place.
        </p>

